diff -ruN oldlinux/Documentation/fb/fbcondecor.rst newlinux/Documentation/fb/fbcondecor.rst
--- oldlinux/Documentation/fb/fbcondecor.rst	1970-01-01 01:00:00.000000000 +0100
+++ newlinux/Documentation/fb/fbcondecor.rst	2026-05-05 09:58:20.836896366 +0200
@@ -0,0 +1,153 @@
+==========
+Fbcondecor
+==========
+
+What is it?
+===========
+
+The framebuffer decorations are a kernel feature which allows displaying a
+background picture on selected consoles and adjust margins when text is
+displayed.
+
+What do I need to get it to work?
+=================================
+
+To get fbcondecor up-and-running and actually doing something you will have
+to:
+
+1. Build your kernel with the FBCONDECOR option enabled.
+2. Get a copy of fbcondecorctl [#f1]_ or a similar program.
+3. Follow the instructions for fbcondecorctl.
+
+.. [#f1] https://github.com/nytelytee/fbcondecorctl
+
+The information below is mostly technical stuff. There's probably no need to
+read it unless you plan to develop a userspace program like fbcondecorctl.
+
+The fbcondecor ioctl
+====================
+
+The fbcondecor ioctl defines a communication interface between the kernel and
+the userspace control program.
+
+The kernel side is responsible for:
+
+- rendering console text, using an image as a background (instead of a
+  standard solid color fbcon uses) or margins
+- accepting commands from the user via the ioctl on the fbcondecor device
+
+The userspace program simply sends an image or margins to the kernel through
+the ioctl.
+
+The userspace program defines the following header::
+
+  #ifndef _UAPI_LINUX_FBCONDECOR_H
+  #define _UAPI_LINUX_FBCONDECOR_H
+
+  #include <linux/types.h>
+  #include <linux/fb.h>
+
+  // Geometry
+  struct fbcondecor_geometry {
+  	__u16 offset_x, offset_y; /* Top left corner  */
+  	__u16 width, height;      /* Width and height of the text field */
+  };
+  
+  // The command that is sent to the ioctl
+  // get/set_image is a raw buffer of size 4*fb_width*fb_height,
+  // when setting, the image is in the framebuffer's native format
+  struct fbcondecor_command
+  {
+  	__u16 flag;         /* The type of the pointer for a command */
+  	__u16 vc;           /* The console to target with the ioctl */
+  	__u32 pad;
+  	__u64 get_geometry; /* Pointer to buffer, or NULL if not getting */
+  	__u64 set_geometry; /* Pointer to geometry, or NULL if turning off */
+  	__u64 get_image;    /* Pointer to buffer, or NULL if not getting */
+  	__u64 set_image;    /* Pointer to image, or NULL if turning off */
+  };
+  
+  // Flag to send to the ioctl
+  #define FBCONDECOR_FLAG_QUERY 0
+  #define FBCONDECOR_FLAG_GET_GEOMETRY 1
+  #define FBCONDECOR_FLAG_GET_IMAGE 2
+  #define FBCONDECOR_FLAG_SET_GEOMETRY 4
+  #define FBCONDECOR_FLAG_SET_IMAGE 8
+  
+  // The response from the ioctl, can also be treated as a flag
+  #define FBCONDECOR_STATE_OFF 0
+  #define FBCONDECOR_STATE_GEOMETRY 1
+  #define FBCONDECOR_STATE_IMAGE 2
+  #define FBCONDECOR_STATE_FULL 3
+
+  #define FBIO_CONDECOR _IOWR('F', 0x19, struct fbcondecor_command)
+
+  #endif
+
+The FBIO_CONDECOR ioctl takes an ``fbcondecor_command`` and acts the following
+way:
+
+1.  If fbcondecor is not initialized, ``-EINVAL`` is returned.
+2.  If the ``fbcondecor_command`` copy from user fails,
+    ``-EFAULT`` is returned.
+3.  If pad is non-zero, ``-EINVAL`` is returned.
+4.  If the vc passed is not allocated, ``-EINVAL`` is returned.
+5.  The console is locked (if any errors occur, it is unlocked prior to
+    returning).
+6.  If the framebuffer dimensions would cause an overflow
+    when multiplying ``4*width*height`` to calculate the image buffer size,
+    ``-EINVAL`` is returned.
+7.  If ``flag & FBCONDECOR_FLAG_GET_GEOMETRY``, but ``get_geometry`` is
+    ``NULL``,
+    ``-EINVAL`` is returned.
+8.  If ``flag & FBCONDECOR_FLAG_GET_IMAGE``, but ``get_image`` is ``NULL``,
+    ``-EINVAL`` is returned.
+9.  The current state of the console decorations is saved; this is what will
+    be returned in case of no errors; it is one of the macros prefixed with
+    ``FBCONDECOR_STATE_`` above.
+10. If ``flag & FBCONDECOR_FLAG_SET_GEOMETRY``, new memory is allocated if
+    the provided geometry is not ``NULL``. If the copy from userspace fails,
+    ``-EFAULT`` is returned. If the geometry passed does not
+    fit inside of the framebuffer, ``-EINVAL`` is returned.
+11. If ``flag & FBCONDECOR_FLAG_SET_IMAGE``, new memory is allocated if
+    the provided image is not ``NULL``. If the copy from userspace fails,
+    ``-EFAULT`` is returned.
+12. If ``flag & FBCONDECOR_FLAG_GET_GEOMETRY`` and the old geometry is not
+    ``NULL``, the old geometry is copied to ``get_geometry``.
+    If this fails, ``-EFAULT`` is returned.
+13. If ``flag & FBCONDECOR_FLAG_GET_IMAGE`` and the old image is not
+    ``NULL``, the old image is copied to ``get_image``.
+    If this fails, ``-EFAULT`` is returned.
+14. If ``flag & FBCONDECOR_FLAG_SET_GEOMETRY``, the geometry on the vc is
+    set to the provided ``set_geometry`` (or removed if ``NULL`` was
+    provided). The console is also resized accordingly.
+15. If ``flag & FBCONDECOR_FLAG_SET_IMAGE``, the image on the vc is
+    set to the provided ``set_image`` (or removed if ``NULL`` was
+    provided).
+16. If the targeted vc is the currently active one, the screen is redrawn.
+17. The console is unlocked.
+18. The previous state (from step 9) is returned.
+
+You may notice that, if you have a ``GET`` flag set, but the state you
+are getting is ``NULL``, your buffer will remain unchanged. You have to
+check the return value of the ioctl to know if you received any data.
+
+Also notice that if you need to set both the geometry and the image at once,
+you should do so, because that triggers only one screen redraw, as opposed
+to setting them separately.
+
+Credit
+======
+
+Original 'bootsplash' project & implementation by:
+  Volker Poplawski <volker@poplawski.de>,
+  Stefan Reinauer <stepan@suse.de>,
+  Steffen Winterfeldt <snwint@suse.de>,
+  Michael Schroeder <mls@suse.de>,
+  Ken Wimer <wimer@suse.de>.
+
+Fbcondecor, fbcondecor protocol design, old implementation & docs by:
+  Michal Januszewski <michalj+fbcondecor@gmail.com>
+
+Fbcondecor, fbcondecorctl, parts of the new implementation & docs by:
+  NyteLyte <nytelyte@tuta.io>
